This is version 3.0 of the DIS Network Library for SGIs from the Computer
Science Department of the Naval Postgraduate School. The design of this
all-new version was Paul Barham's; John Locke implemented the design. Email
addresses are:

	barham@cs.nps.navy.mil
	jxxl@cs.nps.navy.mil

The library uses twin circular buffers to hold incoming PDUs. The net read
process puts new PDUs into one buffer; the application process reads PDUs
from the other. When the application has exhausted its buffer, it forces a
buffer swap, limiting resource contention to the brief time required to switch
buffer state. Following is greater detail in how each process operates.

[Note: test_it.c includes a sample application interface to the lib.]

THE NET READ PROCESS

The net read process is created by a sproc() call made in the application
call net_open(). The entire functionality of the net reader is contained in
the receiver_process(). The net reader reads each new PDU into a temporary
buffer, acquires the semaphore, and copies the PDU into the first available
slot in the current buffer. (Note that the new PDU cannot be read directly
into the current buffer. The recvfrom() blocks indefinitely awaiting a new
PDU to arrive. If none arrived *and* the application exhausted its buffer,
the entire application would deadlock until a new PDU arrived. Therefore,
the recvfrom() must be outside of the semaphore bracketed code.) This is
the basic procedure:

    Loop infinitely

	Perform blocked read on broadcast socket into temporary buffer
	Reject broadcast from self (performed by net_write() in app)
	Reject any PDU types not on receive list (set in app)

	Acquire semaphore
	If buffer swap requested by application switch buffer state
	Copy PDU from temporary buffer into first available slot in
	  current buffer
	Save buffer state in case buffers swapped while awaiting next PDU
	Update buffer state in anticipation of next read
	Release semaphore

THE APPLICATION PROCESS

The application process consists of net_open(), net_read(), net_write(),
net_close() and the application itself. Sole responsibility for swapping
buffers resides in net_read(). Network functionality is initialized with
net_open():

    net_open()

	Initialize shared memory arena
	Open one socket each for sending and receiving broadcasts
	Spawn (sproc()) the receiving process

	Note: the second arg to net_open() sets the size (in PDUs) of
	  the PDU buffers. If the application consumes PDUs at the rate
	  the net produces them, buffer size is not critical. If there
	  are periods of non-consumption, the net read buffer will fill
	  and begin to overwrite the oldest PDUs which are irretrievably
	  lost. Larger buffers will help if the application has the
	  ability to catch up to the rate of inflowing PDUs.

    net_read()

	If swap buffer flag set, swap buffers
	If application PDU buffer empty, swap buffers
	  Else, return ptr to oldest PDU and update buffer state
	If application PDU buffer still empty, return NULL ptr
	  Else, return ptr to oldest PDU and update buffer state

	Note: net_read() takes a ptr to:

	  struct readstat {
	     PDUType      rs_type;
	     int          rs_wasted;
	     int          rs_swap_buffers_flag;
	  };

	  rs_type: PDU type
	  rs_wasted: number of overwritten PDUs when this buffer was
	    under control of the net read process; this number will not
	    change until buffers are swapped again
	  rs_swap_buffers_flag: if set to TRUE by application, swap
	    buffers before checking for PDUs; this results in any
	    additional PDUs in buffer controlled by app to be lost

    net_write()

	Fill in PDU header, including validating length
	Broadcast PDU

    net_close()

	Close socket descriptors
	Kill receiver process
---
